# Performance rules

The hot paths are: rendering a query to SQL, creating the command and its parameters, and reading rows into results (`docs/design.ja.md`). Building the expression objects of a query may allocate; the work per row does not.

A claim that a rule was followed, or that a change is faster, is a measurement (`.cursor/rules/bench.mdc`), not a reading of the code.

## Reading rows

- The work per row does not allocate beyond the result itself (the row object, its strings, its arrays). No boxing of column values, no `object[]` per row, no dictionary lookups by column name per row, no delegate allocated per row.
- Columns are read by ordinal with the typed getters the generator chose. Name-to-ordinal resolution, when a raw query needs it, happens once per reader, not per row.
- Streaming (`IAsyncEnumerable<T>`) holds one row at a time.

## Rendering SQL

- SQL is written into a pooled buffer (`ArrayPool<char>.Shared` or a reusable builder), not built by `string` concatenation or interpolation.
- No LINQ, `params` arrays, or capturing lambdas inside the renderer and the binder. A `static` lambda is fine.
- No `foreach` over an interface (`IReadOnlyList<T>`, `IEnumerable<T>`) inside the renderer: each loop allocates an enumerator. Loop with an index. On #111 the scope check of each column allocated 32 B this way. Arrays, `List<T>`, and `ImmutableArray<T>` have enumerators that do not allocate.
- No `Dictionary` for a map that holds a few entries per statement (the names of its parameters): search an array, and make the dictionary only past a threshold (16 on #111), so a long `IN` list does not become quadratic. On #111 the dictionary of parameter names cost 152 B per SELECT.
- Number formatting in SQL text uses `CultureInfo.InvariantCulture` (and is rare: values are parameters).

## Dispatch and generics

- Per-type data is a `static class Cache<T>` field, never a `Dictionary<Type, ...>` lookup on a hot path. JIT-constant checks (`typeof(T) == typeof(int)`) choose fast paths at no cost.
- The dialect is a type parameter where that lets the JIT and NativeAOT specialize the renderer; a virtual call per clause is acceptable, a virtual call per character is not.

## Fast path and slow path

- Throwing lives in a separate method marked `[DoesNotReturn]` (`ThrowHelper`), so the caller stays small enough to inline.
- Rare work (growing a buffer) lives in a `[MethodImpl(MethodImplOptions.NoInlining)]` method.
- `[MethodImpl(MethodImplOptions.AggressiveInlining)]` only where a measurement shows an effect, and the PR says which.

## Claims

Do not add caching, pooling, SIMD, or unsafe code for speed unless a measurement shows the path is the bottleneck.

```csharp
// BAD — boxes every value and looks the column up by name for every row
var row = new User((long)reader["id"], (string)reader["name"]);

// GOOD — ordinals resolved once, typed getters per row
var row = new User(reader.GetInt64(0), reader.GetString(1));
```
